    "enjoin": {
      "word": "ENJOIN",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To lay upon, as an order or command; to give an injunction to; to direct with authority; to order; to charge. High matter thou enjoin'st me. Milton. I am enjoined by oath to observe three things. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Law)",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To prohibit or restrain by a judicial order or decree; to put an injunction on. This is a suit to enjoin the defendants from disturbing the plaintiffs. Kent. a parent enjoins on his children the duty of obedience. But it has also the sense of command; as, the duties enjoined by God in the moral law. \"This word is more authoritative than direct, and less imperious than command.\" Johnson.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To join or unite. [Obs.] Hooker.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
